From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: kernelbase: an application hive is written back soon after it changes, and at exit

Visual Studio 2022 keeps its private registry (privateregistry.bin, loaded
with RegLoadAppKey) open for its whole life, and its VsRegEdit sets a value
and exits with the hive still open. Our application hives were written only
when the hive's root handle was closed, so nothing either of them set was
ever kept: the file stayed empty, every start rebuilt the configuration, and
the documented first-launch switch (VsRegEdit set ... HKLM Profile
DisableFirstLaunchDialog dword 1) was lost.

As Windows' lazy writer does, a changed hive is now written about a second
after the change (a subtree change notification on the hive's root, waited
on in the thread pool), and every changed hive is written at a normal process
exit. A hive is written to FILE.sgsave and renamed over FILE, so a process
killed while saving leaves the last whole copy.

Gate: test-apphive (exit with the hive open; killed seconds after a change;
whole file; no temporary left). Mutants SG_MUTANT_APPHIVE_NO_EXIT_SAVE,
SG_MUTANT_APPHIVE_NO_LAZY_SAVE.

